<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kabardians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kabardians are the eastern branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a>, living chiefly in Kabardino-Balkaria in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>. From the 15th century their princes ruled the plains of Kabarda along the Terek and its tributaries and held sway over neighbouring mountain peoples. Allied with Muscovy from the 1550s, they were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in the late 18th and early 19th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kabardians.jpg" alt="Kabardians" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liptink0 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kabardia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kabardians, or Kabardinians, are one of the twelve major Circassian tribes, each represented by one of the twelve stars on the green-and-gold Circassian flag. They are also known as Kabardin, Kebertei or Kabarday. Along with the Besleney tribe they speak a distinctive dialect of Circassian. Historically they lived in Kabardia, a region of the north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, which partly corresponds to the present Russian republic of Kabardino-Balkaria.</p><p>Soviet administrative divisions placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> under four designations and political units: Adygeans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adygea">Adygea</a>, Cherkessians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachay-cherkessia">Karachay-Cherkessia</a>, Kabardians in Kabardino-Balkaria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapsugs">Shapsugs</a> in Krasnodar Krai. The four groups are essentially the same people, and the Cherkessians are mostly of the Kabardian and Besleney tribes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Religions historically practised by Kabardians include the native Adyghe Xabze faith, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Kabardians also formed one of the earliest Christian communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, converting in the late 2nd and early 3rd centuries. Some Roman Catholic Kabardians may descend from families who reportedly converted from Orthodoxy during the 13th century. The majority had converted to Islam by the early 19th century, and most Kabardians today are either non-denominational or Sunni Muslims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a>. A small share still follows traditional Xabze beliefs, and Kabardians in the Mozdoksky District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-ossetia-alania">North Ossetia</a> and parts of Stavropol Krai are Orthodox Christians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Kabardians are the largest Circassian group in the world and the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and other countries. In 2002 around 520,000 lived in Kabardino-Balkaria and about 50,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachay-cherkessia">Karachay-Cherkessia</a>. More than a million live in Turkey, concentrated on the Uzunyayla plateau of Kayseri Province and around central Turkey, with villages also in the north-west and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast. Significant populations live in Jordan, and there are communities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>Kabardians have also entered the wider history of the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-temryukovna">Maria Temryukovna</a> became tsaritsa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as the wife of Ivan the Terrible, and Alexey Cherkassky served as Chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> under Empress Elizabeth. Bidar Kadın was imperial consort of Abdul Hamid II, and Servetseza Kadın the first wife of Abdulmejid I of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kabardians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the February Revolution of 1917, a congress at Vladikavkaz founded the Union of United Mountaineers of the North Caucasus and Dagestan, bringing together Chechens, Ingush, Ossetians, <b>Kabardians</b>, Dagestanis and others.</blockquote><a href="/state/mountainous-republic-of-the-northern-caucasus">Mountainous Republic of the Northern Caucasus</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kabardians</b> bred famous horses and set standards of etiquette admired across the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kabarda">Principality of Kabarda</a></li><li><blockquote>It covered the lands of the Terek Cossack Host and of the Chechens, Ingush, Ossetians and <b>Kabardians</b>, with its government at Pyatigorsk and Vladikavkaz.</blockquote><a href="/state/terek-soviet-republic">Terek Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
